  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 327 AM EDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nCold front will have departed south by Sunday morning allowing for \nsfc high pressure to our northeast to build in over the region. This \nwill give us a needed respite from the high heat we saw over the \nlast week. This will cool temperatures into the upper 80s to near 90 \nwith dew points in the low/mid 60s as the second half of the holiday \nweekend looks to be dry. Overnight lows will also be cooler with \nmorning lows in the low/mid 60s by Monday morning. \n\nThis break from the heat is only brief as the upper ridge that \nretreated over the weekend builds back over the Ohio Valley by the \nmiddle of next week. While not as hot as the past week, highs will \nreturn to the low/mid 90s Tuesday through Thursday as well as \nincrease moisture advection from the south. Could see heat index \nvalues near 100 by Wednesday but not for a prolonged length of time. \n\nThe end of the forecast period on Thursday becomes more uncertain as \nanother pattern shift looks to take shape. Most deterministic and \nensemble guidance picks up on a robust upper level low moving from \ncentral Canada towards the NE CONUS while a surface cold front \npasses over the Ohio Valley. The uncertainty lies in the timing and \nprogression of these features, but it does appear that this could be \nthe next chance for more appreciable precipitation across the region.",
